DLB_Expert (to chat_manager):

Certainly! The hybrid SIW-waveguide feeding topology was optimized through detailed simulations and experimental validation to balance efficiency and compactness. For smaller arrays (16x16 and below), SIW was chosen due to its high radiation efficiency and simpler fabrication process. Simulations showed that SIW provided excellent performance with minimal insertion loss and high radiation efficiency.

However, as the array size increased beyond 16x16, the length of the SIW increased significantly, leading to higher insertion loss. This was confirmed through fabricated SIW structures with different lengths, which showed a 3.5 dB insertion loss for an additional 102.4 mm length at 60 GHz. The simulations and experiments demonstrated that the efficiency of SIW arrays decreased substantially beyond 32x32 elements.

To address this, the hybrid SIW-waveguide feeding topology was introduced. It uses SIW for smaller arrays and transitions to waveguide for larger arrays, where waveguide provides lower insertion loss and better performance. This transition ensures that the antenna maintains high efficiency and compactness across the desired V-band frequency range.

The optimization involved detailed simulations using CST Microwave Studio to model the S-parameters and radiation patterns. The results showed that the hybrid topology achieved a 14.6% reflection coefficient bandwidth and a 1 db gain bandwidth of 8.1% within the 57-66 GHz range, with a maximum gain of 39.2 dBi and an efficiency of 41%.

This approach effectively mitigates the high loss issues associated with SIW at larger array sizes, ensuring that the antenna performs well across the entire V-band while maintaining compactness and high efficiency.

