 To maintain consistent Line-of-Sight (LOS) connectivity for Air-to-Air (A2A) links in dynamically changing flight formations, adaptive beamforming strategies are essential. Dynamic Beamforming (DBF) and Multi-User Beamforming (MUBF) can adapt to the rapidly changing channel conditions, focusing the beam towards the aircraft and minimizing interference.

Leveraging GPS for synchronization among network nodes can improve coordination and ensure contention-free transmissions, which are crucial for A2A communication systems. However, this approach may increase complexity and require precise timing synchronization, which can be challenging in high-velocity environments.

Beam-space multiplexing schemes could enhance capacity and improve LOS connectivity by simultaneously serving multiple aircraft with separate beams. However, the non-stationary spatial channel properties in Extremely Large Aperture (ELAA) systems require the development of dynamic beamforming schemes to account for the visibility regions where channel energy is significant.

Research on channel modeling, system-level performance evaluation, and practical implementation for ELAA systems is crucial to overcome the challenges of ensuring consistent LOS connectivity in dynamic A2A environments.

